-- the-library-checks (2026-11-18, found while writing chem/identity.cub): `make library` refuses
-- lib/library.cub. Every one of lib/derive.cub's 24 definitions (dfield-kind, prim-eq-op, dtel-kinds,
-- dshape, nctors, count-rec, bool-d, c-true, c-false, c-and, clams, derive-eq, all-shapes,
-- outer-branches, inner-branches, compare-fields, list-d, c-nil, c-cons, c-app, prim-to-word,
-- derive-show, show-branches, show-fields) is written without a signature, in the raw style of the
-- files the checker is built from. The checker allows that only in the two fixed lists of
-- src/syntax.c: BOOT_RAW (prelude, code, match, nav, cost, lin, check) may hold a definition without a
-- checked signature, BOOT_TRUSTED (prim, std) may hold primitive, foreign or postulate, and the
-- comment above them states that neither list is extended to admit new code. lib/derive.cub is in
-- neither, so it is refused wherever it loads, and because lib/library.cub imports it the library does
-- not check and no file written over the library can be written at all: `hyperactive write` keeps a
-- piece only if nothing newly fails where the change is loaded, and everything after derive.cub in the
-- load order fails already.
-- What the work is: give each of those definitions a signature that checks. Their vocabulary is the
-- checker's own code representation (clam, ccase, cvar, cprim, centry, ctors-at, is-rec-ty, tval,
-- lsnoc, %dent, %view, %gval, the telescope's tx/ty/tz and the type shapes tword/tdata/tg), all of it
-- declared in the raw files without signatures, so the types those definitions are to be stated at
-- have to be named first — which is the same question as what the code representation's type is, and
-- is therefore core work, not a repair of derive.cub alone. The alternative, extending BOOT_RAW, is
-- refused by the core's own statement of the rule.
-- Scoped further (2026-11-18): the obstacle is one level below derive.cub. Code is fully typed — a data
-- declaration with every constructor's fields typed — but Tel, the telescope that derive.cub walks, is
-- declared in check.cub as `data Tel = tx A nm r | ty nm r | tz e`, with no types on any field, and the
-- same holds of the other raw declarations derive.cub reads (ctors-at, centry, is-rec-ty, tval). A
-- signature for dfield-kind or dtel-kinds therefore cannot be written until the telescope
-- representation itself is stated at types: the work is to type that representation in check.cub, and
-- derive.cub's 24 signatures follow from it. That is why this node is stated as the library checking and
-- not as a repair of one file.
-- Until it is done, a file written over the library cannot be written: chem/identity.cub loads after
-- lib/core.cub alone and carries no truncation and no unchosen enumeration for that reason
-- (chemistry-knowledge-graph).
the-library-checks : Node
the-library-checks = node "The library checks: every definition of lib/derive.cub stated at a signature, so that lib/library.cub is accepted and a file can be written over the library again." (cons the-product nil)
